# Line types: scale_linetype

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

economics_long = Giggly.Datasets.economics_long()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`scale_linetype()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/scale_linetype.html), which covers
`scale_linetype_binned()`, `scale_linetype_continuous()` and `scale_linetype_discrete()` too, in
the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An
example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the
page is also a list of what remains.

`scale_linetype()` maps a discrete column to line patterns, solid, dashed, dotted and others, from a
default set supplied by Richard Pearson of the University of Manchester. A continuous column can be
mapped to line types only through `scale_linetype_binned()`, which cuts it into bins first, and
since patterns have no order even that is not advised. A line type can be named by number, by name
or by a string of hex digits, and a missing one is drawn blank. Giggly has
`Giggly.Plot.scale_linetype_discrete/2` and `Giggly.Plot.scale_linetype_manual/2`, a line type
named by number, by name as an atom or as a dash pattern, a tuple of lengths, but not ggplot2's
short name, `scale_linetype()`, nor binned or identity line type scales (`scale_linetype_binned()`,
`scale_linetype_identity()`), and by default maps `linetype` to a discrete column as ggplot2's
default scale does, and draws the legend that reads it back. A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG
document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines
`render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the cell with `Kino.Image`. The data is
`Giggly.Datasets.economics_long/0`, ggplot2's `economics_long`: five US economic time series, a row
for each month and series, which the setup cell binds to `economics_long`, ggplot2's name for it.

## A line type for each series

```r
base <- ggplot(economics_long, aes(date, value01))
base + geom_line(aes(group = variable))
base + geom_line(aes(linetype = variable))
```

`value01` is each series rescaled to run from 0 to 1, so all five share an axis.

```elixir
base = Plot.new(economics_long, %{x: :date, y: :value01})

base
|> Plot.geom_line(%{group: :variable})
|> render.()
```

```elixir
base
|> Plot.geom_line(%{linetype: :variable})
|> render.()
```

## Line types by hand

```r
# See scale_manual for more flexibility
```

The comment points to ggplot2's `scale_manual` page, where `scale_linetype_manual()` gives each
value a pattern chosen by hand. Giggly's is `Giggly.Plot.scale_linetype_manual/2`.

## Common line types

A table of common patterns, as a factor whose levels keep the order they are written in, each
drawn as a thick horizontal line in a panel of its own. `scale_linetype_identity()` draws each
line in the pattern its value names, and `theme_void(20)` draws no axes, grid or background, at a
base font size of 20.

```r
# Common line types ----------------------------
df_lines <- data.frame(
  linetype = factor(
    1:4,
    labels = c("solid", "longdash", "dashed", "dotted")
  )
)
ggplot(df_lines) +
  geom_hline(aes(linetype = linetype, yintercept = 0), linewidth = 2) +
  scale_linetype_identity() +
  facet_grid(linetype ~ .) +
  theme_void(20)
```

Not in Giggly yet: horizontal lines (`geom_hline()`), `scale_linetype_identity()` and
`facet_grid()`.